Introduction
Most anomaly filters in trading code start with a single threshold on a single number: skip the bar if its range is greater than three ATRs, stand aside if volume is above the 95th percentile. Rules like that work until the market changes gear. A range of two ATRs at the London open is routine. The same range at three in the morning is something else. A global cut-off cannot tell the difference, because it measures how far a bar sits from the whole crowd, not from the bars that actually resemble it. Session-aware thresholds, volatility regimes and other local methods address the same gap too; LOF is one route among several, chosen here for how directly it turns "unfamiliar neighbourhood" into a single number.
Local Outlier Factor (LOF) is a general-purpose way to spot points that sit in a thinner neighbourhood than their neighbours do, and it fits this problem well even though nothing in it was built with trading sessions or market regimes specifically in mind. It scores a point by comparing how densely packed its own neighbourhood is with how densely packed the neighbourhoods of its neighbours are. A point in a thin region next to thick ones scores well above 1. A point in a busy region scores about 1, wherever that region happens to sit in the feature space, so a quiet regime and a wild regime can both be "normal" at the same time.
In this article we build LOF from scratch in native MQL5 using the built-in matrix and vector types. No DLLs, ALGLIB, or ONNX are used. Every closed bar becomes a five-dimensional point. The last 500 bars form a reference cloud, and the newest bar is scored against that cloud without ever being added to it. The alarm level is not a hand-picked number: it is a quantile of the reference cloud's own scores, so it moves with the symbol and the timeframe.
We then use the score as an entry gate for a plain EMA-cross Expert Advisor. We also add an indicator that plots the score under the price chart and a script that verifies the engine against independently computed values. Finally we run the gated and ungated EAs on four symbol/timeframe pairs and look at exactly which trades the gate removed.
Scope.
The base strategy is deliberately simple and sits close to breakeven, so that any change caused by the gate is easy to see. We study what the LOF gate does to that strategy. It is not offered as a profitable trading system. All backtests use 1-minute OHLC modelling, cover a single historical interval with no out-of-sample or walk-forward verification, and are a demonstration of the gate's effect rather than a validated trading result.
