Architecture and the LOF Math
The design has two lanes that share one scaler and one reference cloud. The reference lane runs every fourth bar, while the query lane runs on every newly closed bar. The reference lane loads the last 500 closed bars, turns each into five features, fits a robust scaler on them, and lets the engine work out the nearest-neighbour geometry of the cloud. The query lane builds the same five features, applies the scaler fitted in the reference lane, and asks the engine how unfamiliar the point looks against the cloud. If the score is above the alarm level, the EA skips new entries on that bar.
LOF Pipeline: Reference Lane and Query Lane
Fig. 1. The reference lane (top) is rebuilt every fourth bar. The query lane (bottom) runs on every new closed bar and reuses the scaler, the reference cloud and the alarm level from the last refit. Dashed arrows show what the query lane borrows from the reference lane.
The quantities behind the score.
Pick a neighbourhood size k. For a point p, the k-distance is the distance to its k-th nearest neighbour. Equivalently, it is the radius of the smallest ball around p that contains k other points. The reachability distance from p to a neighbour o is the larger of o's own k-distance and the plain distance between p and o. The local reachability density of p is the inverse of the average reachability distance from p to its k neighbours. LOF itself is the average density of p's neighbours divided by p's own density. In formulas:
d_k(p)         = distance from p to its k-th nearest neighbour
N_k(p)         = the k nearest neighbours of p
reach_k(p, o)  = max( d_k(o), d(p, o) )
lrd_k(p)       =
1
/ ( mean over o in N_k(p) of reach_k(p, o) )
LOF_k(p)       = ( mean over o in N_k(p) of lrd_k(o) ) / lrd_k(p)
The reachability step is what keeps the estimate stable. Inside a tight cluster the plain distances between neighbours are tiny and jumpy, and a ratio of them would be noisy. Flooring each distance at the neighbour's own k-distance makes every point in a cluster "reach" the others at the cluster's typical spacing, so the density stops reacting to accidental near-collisions.
Reading the score.
A score near 1 says the point is about as dense as the neighbours it borrows from. A score of 2 says those neighbours live in space twice as dense as the point's own surroundings. Both sides of the ratio are densities measured in the same units, so multiplying every coordinate by the same factor leaves the score unchanged. It is a statement about shape, not about distance. The only thing that breaks this invariance is a tiny guard constant added to the mean reachability distance before it is inverted, which matters at about the ninth decimal.
A twelve-point example.
The self-test script described later uses twelve points in two dimensions: six in a tight cluster near the origin, five in a looser cluster around (4, 4), and one isolated point at (2.1, 7.9). With k = 3 the origin cluster scores between 0.94 and 1.09, the cluster near (4, 4) between 0.96 and 1.10, and the isolated point 5.49. Scoring three new points against that cloud gives 0.92 for (0, 0), which sits inside the tight cluster, 6.00 for (2, 2), which sits in empty space between the clusters, and 11.71 for (9, -3), which is far from everything. A single distance cut-off measured from the overall centre has to be generous enough for the looser cluster, and by then it tends to miss oddities near the tight one.
Where the alarm level comes from.
After the reference cloud is fitted, every reference point has its own LOF score. The alarm level is a quantile of those scores, 97.5% by default:
alarm_level = quantile( LOF of every reference point,
0.975
)
That 2.5% figure describes the reference scores themselves, not new bars: with interpolation between order statistics and ties in the data, the exact share can land a little off 2.5%, and a new bar is judged as a novelty point against an aging window rather than against itself, so its firing rate is a separate question we measure in the tests. Because the level is read off each symbol's own cloud rather than hard-coded, the same code ran unchanged on gold, EURUSD and another timeframe below without retuning a number such as 1.5 — checked on these four pairs, not established as a general property.
What one refit costs.
The engine measures brute-force distances from each of the 500 reference points to all 500: 500 x 500 x 5 is about 1.25 million multiply-adds, plus a bounded insertion list that keeps the k nearest. That happens once every InpRefitEvery bars. The per-bar query afterwards is only 500 distances.
Keeping the bar out of its own reference
. The reference window always ends at shift 2, one bar before the bar being judged at shift 1. If the bar belonged to the cloud it is measured against, it would raise the local density around itself, and unusual bars would look less unusual than they are.
MarketFeatures.mqh: From Bars to Points
LOF only ever sees distances, so what the coordinates mean and how they are scaled decides everything the engine does afterwards. MarketFeatures.mqh defines five features per bar, loads a whole window of bars in a single pass, fits a robust scaler on the reference window, and applies that same scaler to every new bar.
Two constants sit at the top of the file. MF_DIM fixes the width of the feature vector, and MF_EPS is the floor used when ATR values and scales are checked before a division.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Constants                                                        |
//| Width of the feature vector built for every bar, and the numeric |
//| floor that protects divisions inside the robust scaler.          |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
#define
MF_DIM
5
// features per bar
#define
MF_EPS
1.0e-12
// floor for scale and ATR checks
The five column positions live in an enum, so every matrix built by the class can be addressed by name instead of by a magic number.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| ENUM_MF_FEATURE                                                  |
//| Column positions inside one feature vector. Every matrix built   |
//| by CMarketFeatures uses this order, so a column can be addressed |
//| by name instead of a magic number.                               |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
enum
ENUM_MF_FEATURE
  {
   MF_F_RETURN,
// log return close[t] / close[t-1]
MF_F_RANGE,
// bar range divided by the previous bar's ATR
MF_F_VOLUME,
// log(1 + tick volume)
MF_F_SPREAD,
// spread in points recorded on the bar
MF_F_CLOSELOC
// close location inside the bar, centred on zero
};
The features
.
Feature 0 is the log return of the close. It is additive across bars and symmetric around zero, unlike a percentage change. Feature 1 is the bar's range in ATR units, so a big bar means the same thing on gold and on EURUSD, which is what lets one alarm rule survive a change of symbol. The ATR used is the one from the bar before, because the bar's own range is part of its own ATR, and the goal is to compare the bar with conditions that existed before it formed. Feature 2 is log(1 + tick volume): volume is heavy-tailed and the log compresses it. Feature 3 is the spread in points recorded on the bar, since a widening spread is one of the first symptoms of an unusual market state. Feature 4 is where the bar closed inside its own range, shifted so that 0 means the middle, with a neutral 0 for zero-range bars. None of the five features names the time of day or session directly, so the gate relies on volatility, volume and spread to carry that signal rather than a clock — a calendar feature is a natural addition, not something this version does.
The class stores configuration (symbol, timeframe, ATR handle, clip level) and scaler state (per-feature centre and scale, plus a fitted flag). Init creates the ATR handle once. BuildReference and LoadQuery are the two loaders the EA and the indicator call. FitScaler, Transform and TransformMatrix are public so they can be reused and tested on their own.
