FitScaler: median and MAD, not mean and standard deviation.
The reference window will contain some of the very bars we want to flag. A mean and a standard deviation get dragged by them, while the median and the median absolute deviation barely move. The factor 1.4826 rescales the MAD so that it equals the standard deviation for normally distributed data, which keeps the standardized values on a familiar scale. A feature that is constant for more than half the window has a MAD of zero, for example the spread on a fixed-spread account. The function then falls back to the mean absolute deviation, so a rare spike stays large but finite, and as a last resort to a scale of 1.0, so a perfectly constant feature contributes nothing to any distance.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CMarketFeatures::FitScaler                                       |
//| Fits a robust scaler on the reference window: per feature, the   |
//| center is the median and the scale is 1.4826 * MAD, which equals |
//| the standard deviation for normal data but ignores outliers.     |
//|                                                                  |
//| Fallbacks keep the scale usable when a feature barely moves:     |
//| first the mean absolute deviation, then 1.0. The scaler is       |
//| fitted on reference data only and reused for every later query.  |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
bool
CMarketFeatures::FitScaler(
matrix
&raw)
  {
int
n = (
int
)raw.Rows();
// reference rows
double
col[];
// scratch copy of one feature column
double
dev[];
// absolute deviations from the median
//--- Ten rows is the bare minimum for a median and MAD that mean anything.
if
(n <
10
|| (
int
)raw.Cols() != MF_DIM)
return
(
false
);
ArrayResize
(col, n);
ArrayResize
(dev, n);
for
(
int
c =
0
; c < MF_DIM; c++)
     {
for
(
int
i =
0
; i < n; i++)
         col[i] = raw[i][c];
double
med     = MedianOf(col, n);
// column median (also sorts col)
double
sum_dev =
0.0
;
// running sum of absolute deviations
for
(
int
i =
0
; i < n; i++)
        {
         dev[i]   =
MathAbs
(col[i] - med);
         sum_dev += dev[i];
        }
double
mad   = MedianOf(dev, n);
// median absolute deviation
double
scale =
1.4826
* mad;
// MAD rescaled to a standard deviation
//--- A feature that is constant for more than half the window (spread on a
//    fixed-spread account) has MAD = 0. Falling back to the mean absolute
//    deviation keeps a rare spike large but finite, and the last resort of 1.0
//    makes a perfectly constant feature contribute nothing.
if
(scale < MF_EPS)
         scale =
1.2533
* sum_dev / n;
if
(scale < MF_EPS)
