Transform: centre, scale, clip.
Each raw value has the reference median subtracted and is divided by the robust scale. The result is then clipped to plus or minus InpClip, 8.0 by default. Clipping bounds how much any single feature can contribute to a distance. Without it, one freak bar such as a news spike or a bad tick would sit hundreds of units from everything, and every distance involving it would be meaningless. Transform() is public and reusable on its own, so it now guards that precondition directly rather than assuming it.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CMarketFeatures::Transform                                       |
//| Standardizes one raw feature row with the fitted scaler:         |
//| subtract the median, divide by the robust scale, clip to +/-     |
//| m_clip, after checking that FitScaler() has already run. An      |
//| unfit call returns a zero vector instead of using unset state.   |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
void
CMarketFeatures::Transform(
const
double
&raw_row[],
double
&out[])
  {
ArrayResize
(out, MF_DIM);
//--- Guard: Transform() is public and reusable, so a caller that skips BuildReference/
//    LoadQuery must not standardize against an unset scaler.
if
(!m_fitted)
     {
ArrayInitialize
(out,
0.0
);
return
;
     }
for
(
int
c =
0
; c < MF_DIM; c++)
     {
double
z = (raw_row[c] - m_center[c]) / m_scale[c];
//--- Clipping bounds the distance contribution of any single feature. Without it,
//    one freak bar (news spike, bad tick) would sit hundreds of units from
//    everything and make every distance involving it meaningless.
out[c] =
MathMax
(-m_clip,
MathMin
(m_clip, z));
     }
  }
BuildReference: load, fit, transform in one call.
The EA and the indicator never call FitScaler or Transform directly. BuildReference wraps the sequence, so the scaler always ends up fitted on exactly the window the engine will use as its reference. LoadQuery mirrors it for a single bar and refuses to run until a scaler exists. The scaler is never fitted on a query: if it were, every bar would look normal to itself.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CMarketFeatures::BuildReference                                  |
//| Loads 'count' bars ending at 'first_shift', fits the scaler on   |
//| them and returns the standardized matrix in 'std_ref'.           |
//|                                                                  |
//| Callers pass first_shift = 2 (or s + 1 when scoring the bar at   |
//| shift s) so the bar about to be scored is never part of its own  |
//| reference window.                                                |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
