LOFEngine.mqh: Neighbours, Reachability and the Score
LOFEngine.mqh holds the algorithm. It knows nothing about bars, prices or MetaTrader. It takes a matrix of points and returns scores, which is why the same class runs unchanged in the EA, in the indicator and in the self-test script.
One constant lives at the top of the file. LOF_EPS is added to the mean reachability distance before it is inverted. It only matters when several reference points coincide exactly: their reachability distances are zero and the density would otherwise be infinite. It is the same value scikit-learn uses, which lets the self-test compare against that library's numbers.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| Constants                                                        |
//| Small constant added to the mean reachability distance before it |
//| is inverted. It stops the local density from becoming infinite   |
//| when several reference points sit on top of each other.          |
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
#define
LOF_EPS
1.0e-10
// density guard, same value scikit-learn uses
The class holds its configuration (k and the alarm quantile), the fitted reference (the matrix of points, the vectors of k-distance, density and LOF score, and two flat tables with neighbour indices and distances) and one scratch buffer for distances. The two neighbour tables are plain arrays addressed as row * k + rank, which keeps the inner loops simple. Fit and Score are the two calls that matter. Everything else is either a helper or an accessor.
