SelectNearest: a bounded insertion list.
To find the k nearest points, sorting all n distances would cost O(n log n) per point. Instead the function keeps a sorted list of the k best candidates so far. Once the list is full, a candidate that is not closer than the current k-th best fails a single comparison and is skipped, so almost every point costs one comparison. When a candidate does get in, it takes the last slot and bubbles left until the list is ordered again. The exclude argument removes one index, so a reference point never counts as its own neighbour. A new query passes -1 because it is not in the reference at all.
//+------------------------------------------------------------------+
//| CLOFEngine::SelectNearest                                        |
//| Finds the k nearest reference points from a distance array.      |
//| idx[] and val[] (both sized k) receive the neighbour indices and |
//| distances in ascending order. 'exclude' removes one index, so a  |
//| reference point never counts as its own neighbour (-1 for a new  |
//| query). Returns how many neighbours were found.                  |
//|                                                                  |
//| A bounded insertion list is used instead of sorting all n        |
//| distances: after the first k points almost every candidate fails |
//| one comparison against the current k-th best, so the pass costs  |
//| close to O(n) instead of O(n log n).                             |
