Fit: three passes, in this order.
Pass one finds every point's neighbours and k-distance. Pass two computes every point's density, which needs the k-distance of each neighbour, so pass one has to finish for all points first. Pass three computes the LOF scores, which need the density of each neighbour, so it gets its own pass after all densities exist. The reference matrix is copied into the object, so the caller's matrix can be reused freely. The last step sets the alarm level from the reference scores. Fit returns false when the set has k or fewer points, because a point needs k neighbours other than itself.
